Configuring AWS credentials with the AWS CLI
Setting environment variables works well, but they reset when you close your terminal. A more
idx_05f9a5b2 permanent idx_91351ff9 and beginner-friendly approach idx_0da44e39 is to use the AWS CLI. If you have the idx_2c1908e4 AWS CLI
installed (see https://aws.amazon.com/cli/), simply run the following command in your
terminal:
aws configure
It will prompt you for your access key ID, secret access key, default region (e.g., us-east-1),
and output format. It saves these to a credentials file at ~/.aws/credentials (macOS/Linux)
or C:\Users\<you>\.aws\credentials (Windows). Terraform automatically reads this file, so
no further configuration is needed. This method is particularly convenient on Windows, where
setting persistent environment variables requires navigating system properties.
